Declare a `Response` parameter and set its `status_code`. FastAPI still serializes your return value through the response model and then applies the status code (and any headers) you set on that temporary response.

```python
from fastapi import FastAPI, Response, status
from pydantic import BaseModel

app = FastAPI()


class TaskIn(BaseModel):
    title: str


class Task(BaseModel):
    id: str
    title: str


tasks: dict[str, str] = {"t1": "Write docs"}


@app.put(
    "/tasks/{task_id}",
    response_model=Task,
    responses={201: {"model": Task, "description": "Task created"}},
)
async def upsert_task(task_id: str, task: TaskIn, response: Response):
    if task_id not in tasks:
        response.status_code = status.HTTP_201_CREATED
    tasks[task_id] = task.title
    return {"id": task_id, "title": task.title}
```

Updating `t1` returns 200 and creating `t2` returns 201, both with the same body shape. The `responses` entry is only for the docs, so the 201 case shows up in OpenAPI too.

